You are a robotics test analyst. You are given EVIDENCE computed by code from simulated pick-and-place runs of a fixed controller. Explain WHY it fails as a few testable hypotheses. Rules: (1) cite evidence ids (like E7) in evidence_refs for every claim; use only ids that appear below. (2) Do NOT state statistics or counts in your own words: refer to evidence by id, because a person will see the numbers from the evidence table, not from you. (3) region is a machine-checkable predicate: scenario axes with [lo, hi] inside the allowed bounds. (4) levers may only be these tunable parameters: grip_force, grasp_hold_steps, retry_count, lift_speed, lift_height. (5) prediction must be testable, for example which failures should disappear if a lever moves. Return between 1 and 4 hypotheses with ids H1, H2, ... You do not decide pass or fail.

Reply with a single JSON object matching this JSON Schema, and nothing else:
{"$defs": {"Hypothesis": {"additionalProperties": false, "properties": {"id": {"title": "Id", "type": "string"}, "statement": {"minLength": 1, "title": "Statement", "type": "string"}, "region": {"additionalProperties": {"items": {"type": "number"}, "type": "array"}, "title": "Region", "type": "object"}, "mechanism": {"minLength": 1, "title": "Mechanism", "type": "string"}, "levers": {"items": {"$ref": "#/$defs/Lever"}, "minItems": 1, "title": "Levers", "type": "array"}, "prediction": {"minLength": 1, "title": "Prediction", "type": "string"}, "evidence_refs": {"items": {"type": "string"}, "minItems": 1, "title": "Evidence Refs", "type": "array"}}, "required": ["id", "statement", "region", "mechanism", "levers", "prediction", "evidence_refs"], "title": "Hypothesis", "type": "object"}, "Lever": {"additionalProperties": false, "properties": {"param": {"title": "Param", "type": "string"}, "direction": {"enum": ["increase", "decrease"], "title": "Direction", "type": "string"}}, "required": ["param", "direction"], "title": "Lever", "type": "object"}}, "additionalProperties": false, "properties": {"hypotheses": {"items": {"$ref": "#/$defs/Hypothesis"}, "maxItems": 5, "minItems": 1, "title": "Hypotheses", "type": "array"}}, "required": ["hypotheses"], "title": "HypothesisList", "type": "object"}

reply:
{
  "hypotheses": [
    {
      "id": "H1",
      "statement": "Low friction and high mass cause MISSED_GRASP because the current grip_force (15 N) cannot generate enough friction force to lift the cube.",
      "region": {
        "friction": [0.1, 0.34],
        "mass_kg": [0.52, 1.0]
      },
      "mechanism": "The friction force (μ * grip_force) is less than the cube's weight (m*g) during lift, so the cube never leaves the table (MISSED_GRASP).",
      "levers": [
        {
          "param": "grip_force",
          "direction": "increase"
        }
      ],
      "prediction": "If grip_force is increased, MISSED_GRASP failures in the low-friction, high-mass region should decrease significantly.",
      "evidence_refs": ["E8", "E39", "E46", "E47"]
    },
    {
      "id": "H2",
      "statement": "High sensor noise causes MISSED_GRASP because noisy position estimates lead to misaligned grasps, and with retry_count=0 there is no recovery.",
      "region": {
        "sensor_noise_m": [0.02, 0.03]
      },
      "mechanism": "Sensor noise causes the gripper to misjudge the cube's position, resulting in fingers landing on the cube or missing it entirely (MISSED_GRASP). Retries provide fresh sensor readings.",
      "levers": [
        {
          "param": "retry_count",
          "direction": "increase"
        }
      ],
      "prediction": "If retry_count is increased from 0 to 1 or more, MISSED_GRASP failures in high sensor noise regions should decrease.",
      "evidence_refs": ["E31", "E32", "E38", "E42", "E48"]
    },
    {
      "id": "H3",
      "statement": "High gripper_delay_steps cause MISSED_GRASP because the fixed grasp_hold_steps (10) is too short after the delayed command to establish a stable grasp before lifting.",
      "region": {
        "gripper_delay_steps": [12, 16]
      },
      "mechanism": "The gripper command is delayed by gripper_delay_steps. After the command, the controller waits only grasp_hold_steps before lifting. If the delay is long, the cube may have moved or the fingers may not have fully closed before the hold timer starts.",
      "levers": [
        {
          "param": "grasp_hold_steps",
          "direction": "increase"
        }
      ],
      "prediction": "If grasp_hold_steps is increased, MISSED_GRASP failures in high gripper_delay_steps regions should decrease.",
      "evidence_refs": ["E36", "E40", "E41", "E44"]
    },
    {
      "id": "H4",
      "statement": "Small cube size combined with low friction causes MISSED_GRASP because the reduced contact area lowers the effective friction force for a given grip_force.",
      "region": {
        "friction": [0.1, 0.4667],
        "size_m": [0.03, 0.0467]
      },
      "mechanism": "Small cubes have less contact area, so the normal force per finger is lower for a given grip_force, resulting in lower friction force. With low friction coefficient, the grasp cannot hold the cube.",
      "levers": [
        {
          "param": "grip_force",
          "direction": "increase"
        }
      ],
      "prediction": "If grip_force is increased, MISSED_GRASP failures in the small-size, low-friction region should decrease.",
      "evidence_refs": ["E13", "E43", "E46", "E47"]
    }
  ]
}

